#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152058 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2755c3f-e76f-5cf9-a498-333e7e98ea8d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Addition and Subtraction Properties"}] :question/id #uuid "38f80c3a-6e6e-503d-a015-7040edc622bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-14580" :question/problem "If $x + 8 \\equiv 11\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,15),$ find an equivalent statement where $x$ is isolated." :question/worked-solution "The addition property of congruences states that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+c\\equiv b+c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a-c\\equiv b-c \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe use the subtraction property to subtract $8$ from both sides of the given congruence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+8 & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+8-8 & ≡11-8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152059 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "489ba3cf-c7da-5766-9638-2b5c5429d04c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integers That Are Congruent to Zero Modulo N"}] :question/id #uuid "e379f1ee-3de3-56ad-8866-d9189f892704" :question/math-academy-id "e-14582" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers is congruent to $0$ modulo $18?$\n\n- $-45$\n\n- $42$\n\n- $-54$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that  \n\n$$\na \\equiv 0 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad a = kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $18$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $18.$\n\nSo, since $18\\,|\\,(-54),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-54 \\equiv (-3) \\cdot {\\color{blue}18} \\equiv (-3)\\cdot 0\\equiv 0 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}18}).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $18.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152060 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7faa3c23-fa98-5c95-9c09-f0d489dbd472" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Congruences"}] :question/id #uuid "e92680c1-e40a-521d-b5b5-16a801e016ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-14585" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x \\equiv 23\\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)?$ \n\n- $x \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $x \\equiv 3 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$\n\n- $x \\equiv 5 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6)$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  ${\\color{blue}6} \\cdot (-3) \\equiv 0 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{blue}6})$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡23+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x & ≡23+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡23+6\\cdot (-3)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡23-18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151863 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "1da5794f-02be-5a46-9450-d4b5611ee5a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14646" :question/problem "The opening ceremony of the first modern Olympic Games was held on April 6th, 1896. Given that January 1st, 1896, was a Wednesday, use congruences to determine the day on which the Olympic Games opened.\n\n*Hint: The year 1896 was a leap year since it's divisible by $4$ and is not an end-of-century year.*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1896, and April 6th, 1896. \n\n- There were $2$ months with $31$ days (January and March).\n\n- There were $0$ months with $30$ days.\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February) since it was a leap year.\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and April 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd \n= \\overbrace{31(2) + 30(0) + 29(1)}^{\\textrm{days to April 1st}} + 5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31 \\equiv 3, \\qquad 30\\equiv 2, \\qquad 29\\equiv 1 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\, 7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =2(31)+0(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡2(3)+0(2)+1(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6+0+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡12\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $5$ days after January 1st, 1896, namely Monday."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152059 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "489ba3cf-c7da-5766-9638-2b5c5429d04c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integers That Are Congruent to Zero Modulo N"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13926285-aa6b-5092-9d57-fa8e5980190e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410587} {:answer/id #uuid "26fb6187-2ddc-5cb5-90e4-735971aabc57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410597} {:answer/id #uuid "3d250d98-53be-5872-b250-2cfdd5b52870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410606} {:answer/id #uuid "410f4b23-b18f-514c-8538-727b7e177f64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410608} {:answer/id #uuid "defce10e-4a66-50b5-aab9-31620eabe1ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d250d98-53be-5872-b250-2cfdd5b52870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410606} :answer-field/id #uuid "8554aa24-0dd3-5f08-91e8-2982a7c41834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f9b7d3e-524b-551d-8a94-9d846396890c" :question/math-academy-id "q-132085" :question/problem "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $15?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $15$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $15.$\n\nTherefore, since $15\\,|\\,60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n60≡4\\cdot 15≡4\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152059 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "489ba3cf-c7da-5766-9638-2b5c5429d04c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integers That Are Congruent to Zero Modulo N"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3951433c-8ef2-53fb-9fa1-a20d8c2e401f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410604} {:answer/id #uuid "53963393-a2e3-5e41-8999-eeb1f37a839f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410619} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3dfac6-2843-5cc0-80c8-368a37714dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410642} {:answer/id #uuid "e576421c-d593-5aa6-9e50-abdf4fe2585e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410669} {:answer/id #uuid "ef70bf99-fcf0-55ec-928b-12c6c2ca010b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e576421c-d593-5aa6-9e50-abdf4fe2585e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410669} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae42fde0-2dea-5dfb-9792-c0acd061d911" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abd575a7-0f26-5465-b126-ff96772a8759" :question/math-academy-id "q-132087" :question/problem "Which of the following is congruent to $0$ modulo $16?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\na≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a=kn,\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in Z.$ In other words, when $a$ is divided by $n,$ there is no remainder. This means that any integer multiple of $16$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $16.$ \n\nTherefore, since $16\\,|\\,64,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n64≡4\\cdot 16≡4\\cdot 0≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16).\n$$\n\nNone of the other options are multiples of $16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152060 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7faa3c23-fa98-5c95-9c09-f0d489dbd472" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Congruences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26354798-307b-5557-a789-f21a69500cd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410595} {:answer/id #uuid "85c90695-7b13-51e8-ae35-ae006e4c9ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410636} {:answer/id #uuid "a46f14e5-0e1b-50b0-be84-6fffb64d7571" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410647} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a95d9f-7b79-5880-b604-8eec030fc7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410653} {:answer/id #uuid "ed626ba6-cb87-5f12-8f75-aa44a083d800" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85c90695-7b13-51e8-ae35-ae006e4c9ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410636} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6519838-5e86-5791-bf74-fda374f69c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d19c623d-9750-5d15-989b-ed8e917e3481" :question/math-academy-id "q-132093" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)?$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $12\\cdot (-2)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡26+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\ x & ≡26+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡26+12\\cdot (-2)\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡26-24\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152060 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7faa3c23-fa98-5c95-9c09-f0d489dbd472" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Congruences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226134f3-e697-5f75-9604-0da171ad901b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡24\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410593} {:answer/id #uuid "bea184a4-5a95-5a73-a77c-f8431f0b4e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡25\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410654} {:answer/id #uuid "c14a6194-233e-52ea-b81d-817aae45fcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410658} {:answer/id #uuid "e34b7e67-2e2a-5a2d-9934-09874839fbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410668} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ed3293-96fa-5701-98ca-7926f6b2af1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡28\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34b7e67-2e2a-5a2d-9934-09874839fbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≡26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410668} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcbe6109-bc46-5ab0-9f30-3701f2c81e8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bc99e19c-49e9-51e2-8ead-f8afe5651f31" :question/math-academy-id "q-132097" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $x≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "When we add zero to both sides of a congruence modulo $n$, we can form an equivalent congruence by writing zero as an integer multiple of $n.$ This works because every integer multiple of $n$ is congruent to zero modulo $n.$\n\nIn our case, we add zero to both sides of the congruence and then use the fact that  $7\\cdot 3≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ to rewrite the right-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ x & ≡5+0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+7\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡5+21\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡26\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152058 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2755c3f-e76f-5cf9-a498-333e7e98ea8d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Addition and Subtraction Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15c898bd-31e5-598a-9024-2b8ac1db97bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410588} {:answer/id #uuid "b6eeab0e-c9b4-555a-b768-c0d65636b671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410652} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1c5c83-9911-5f49-a5d9-be4b8dc96905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce1c5c83-9911-5f49-a5d9-be4b8dc96905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410662} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2397fde-472a-58bc-822f-72daa055f923" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b852f2e-cc76-5abd-9729-0fa28782630f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410583} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3b024a-6841-53c2-ba86-bfe8723cb5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410615} {:answer/id #uuid "a03073d6-a32c-5694-8477-2997e27e80b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b852f2e-cc76-5abd-9729-0fa28782630f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410583} :answer-field/id #uuid "beb8a709-86ff-5740-9ddc-63976e27facc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e035a9d-3424-502a-9960-8aabd3f51b44" :question/math-academy-id "q-211991" :question/problem "Given that $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The subtraction property states that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-c≡b-c\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can subtract the same integer from both sides of a congruence, keeping the modulus unchanged:\n\n$$\na≡b\\,⟺\\,a-2≡b-2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2695a325-110d-50bc-8dcd-637ddf9e0b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Sunday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410596} {:answer/id #uuid "29c90762-ad00-51c7-858e-4662c5bf0620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410598} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad2aa1e-2756-588f-b7c1-9126054efb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wednesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410629} {:answer/id #uuid "c77205d4-aa6d-56d6-a0da-855fbd3a4628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Saturday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410659} {:answer/id #uuid "de4dc52c-f7a5-523c-96ba-60dae695e897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Monday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77205d4-aa6d-56d6-a0da-855fbd3a4628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Saturday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410659} :answer-field/id #uuid "67fd7e7a-5d04-51e0-a66a-8f26c30b7b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35bbfc39-ec46-5081-a039-066e5953ba2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-212904" :question/problem "You're given that January 1st, 2000, was a Saturday. Use congruences to determine the day of the week on November 4th, 2000.\n\n*Hint: The year 2000 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2000, and November 1st, 2000. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 4th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overset{\\text{days to November 1st}}{\\overset{⏞}{31(6)+30(3)+29(1)}}+3.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(29)+3 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(1)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+1+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡14\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡0\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as January 1st, 2000, namely Saturday."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152058 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2755c3f-e76f-5cf9-a498-333e7e98ea8d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Addition and Subtraction Properties"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd74d72-98fd-5356-b52c-46cdaa8786a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410584} {:answer/id #uuid "d58b22c9-67f2-5e11-806d-b43ed8dabaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410663} {:answer/id #uuid "f9997762-58ee-54b6-bfde-531bd2973ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d58b22c9-67f2-5e11-806d-b43ed8dabaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410663} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e48cdcd-8b74-562f-9634-c4bc88e76046" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answ